**<span class="mark">Am I eligible for weight loss surgery in
Perth?</span>**

<span class="mark">If you have spent years trying to lose weight and
keep finding yourself back where you started, you are not alone, and it
is not a failure of willpower. Obesity is a medical condition and, like
any medical condition, it responds better to proper treatment than to
trying harder. This page walks you through who is eligible for weight
loss surgery in Perth, what your BMI and health history mean in
practice, and what to expect when you meet us.</span>

**<span class="mark">Eligibility is about more than a single
number</span>**

<span class="mark">Most people start by asking whether their BMI is high
enough. It is a fair question, and BMI does form part of the picture.
But eligibility for weight loss surgery is not decided by a single
number. When you meet your Obesity Surgery WA surgeon, they will review
your weight, your whole health history, what you have already tried, and
what you want your life to look like in five or ten years.</span>

<span class="mark">Technically, body mass index (BMI) is your weight in
kilograms divided by your height in metres squared. As a guide for
Australians: a BMI of ~27 to 29 sits in the overweight range, ~30 to 34
is class 1 obesity, ~35 to 39 is class 2 obesity, and ~40 and above is
class 3, sometimes called severe obesity. Your BMI influences which
options are likely to suit you, but it does not tell the whole story on
its own.</span>

<span class="mark">Two people can have the same BMI and receive very
different recommendations. Someone at BMI 32 with type 2 diabetes and
sleep apnoea, for example, may be a strong surgical candidate, while
someone at the same BMI with no related health conditions might be
better placed starting with a medical or dietitian-led program.</span>

| <span class="mark">You do not need to know your BMI before getting in touch; our team can help you work it out, and your surgeon will measure and confirm it at your consultation.</span> |
|----|

### **<span class="mark">Is weight loss surgery suitable for you? </span>**

| <span class="mark">As a general guide, surgical assessment is typically available for those with a BMI of ~35, and almost always from 40 and higher. Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify where weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present and there have been previous attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise, or non-surgical means without lasting success. Please note that these are general clinical indicators only; your surgeon will make a clinical assessment and discuss your options with you when you meet.</span> |
|----|

- <span class="mark">BMI 27-29: surgery is not usually the first
  recommendation at this range. Our medical team can often provide
  meaningful support through GLP-1 medications, structured dietary
  programs, and dietitian-led care.</span>

- <span class="mark">BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition:
  surgery may be appropriate at this range when conditions such as type
  2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, which
  can strengthen the case for a surgical pathway.</span>

- <span class="mark">BMI 35-39: most people in this range are eligible
  for a surgical assessment, with or without other health conditions. A
  range of procedures may be suitable, and your surgeon will discuss the
  options most appropriate for your situation.</span>

- <span class="mark">BMI 40+: almost everyone in this range is eligible
  for surgery and for many patients it represents the most effective
  long-term pathway available. Your surgeon can confirm which procedure
  is the right fit.</span>

- <span class="mark">Weight-related health conditions or comorbidities:
  certain conditions strengthen a surgical case regardless of BMI. These
  include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep
  apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH),
  and severe acid reflux.</span>

- <span class="mark">A history of diets that haven't worked: this is
  expected and very normal, not a mark ‘against’ you. Documented
  attempts at non-surgical weight loss are actually part of what helps
  confirm that a more intensive pathway is appropriate. You are not here
  because you didn't try hard enough.</span>

- <span class="mark">Mental health and psychological readiness:
  psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment process, but it is
  never a reason to be turned away. A psychological review is there to
  set you up for the best possible outcome, not to screen you
  out.</span>

### <span class="mark">**How it works** </span>

<span class="mark">**1. Get in touch — by self-referral or through your
GP  **
You can contact us directly or ask your GP to send a referral. A GP
referral can help with Medicare rebates on consultation fees so it is
worth asking for one, but it is not required to get started.</span>

<span class="mark">**2. Your orientation consultation  **
This first appointment runs 30-60 minutes and is a relaxed, honest
conversation about your weight history, your health, and your goals.
Your MBBS FRACS surgeon will explain which options are likely to suit
you, walk through relevant procedures, and answer your questions. Costs
are discussed openly so you leave knowing exactly where you stand, with
no obligation to proceed.</span>

<span class="mark">**3. Assessment and preparation  **
If you decide to move forward, your surgeon will recommend any relevant
tests and you will meet members of the wider team, which may include a
dietitian and, where appropriate, a psychologist. This stage confirms
that surgery is safe and right for you, and prepares you for the changes
ahead.</span>

<span class="mark">**4. Surgery and aftercare  **
Once everything else is in place, your procedure is scheduled. Surgeries
are performed at [<u>Mount Private
Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private
Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [<u>Waikiki Private
Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Our Perth-based team will
continue to support you through recovery and the months that
follow.</span>

### **<span class="mark">What finding the right pathway looks like in practice Alt Where the right assessment can take you </span>**

1.  <span class="mark">A clear, personalised answer about whether
    surgery is right for you, from a surgeon who knows your full
    picture.</span>

2.  <span class="mark">A pathway tailored to where you are starting
    from, which may include weight loss medication or dietitian-led
    support if surgery is not the right first step.</span>

3.  <span class="mark">For many patients, surgery supports meaningful
    improvement in weight-related health conditions including type 2
    diabetes, sleep apnoea, and high blood pressure, though individual
    results vary.</span>

4.  <span class="mark">Care from MBBS FRACS-certified bariatric
    surgeons, with procedures performed at three leading private
    hospital locations in Perth.</span>

5.  <span class="mark">A team that takes your history of prior weight
    loss attempts seriously and builds a plan around your individual
    circumstances, not a standard template.</span>

### **<span class="mark">What to understand before you decide Alt An honest word on what surgery involves</span>**

Being eligible for weight loss surgery does not automatically make it
the right choice for you. Part of Obesity Surgery WA’s assessment
process is making sure you understand the commitment and the risks
involved so that whatever you decide, you decide with a clear picture of
what’s involved.

- <span class="mark">All surgery carries risk including bleeding,
  infection, blood clots, and adverse reactions to anaesthetic.</span>

- <span class="mark">Bariatric procedures require lifelong changes to
  how you eat and ongoing follow-up with your care team.</span>

- <span class="mark">Some procedures require long-term vitamin and
  mineral supplementation, particularly those that affect nutrient
  absorption such as the gastric bypass.</span>

- <span class="mark">Results depend on adherence to dietary and
  lifestyle guidance after surgery, and outcomes vary between
  individuals.</span>

- <span class="mark">Surgery is a powerful tool, but it works best when
  supported by the habits and care built around it — it is not a
  standalone cure.</span>

### **<span class="mark">Weight loss surgery cost </span>**

<span class="mark">The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies
depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your
hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate
private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a
significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates
apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private
health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing
options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your
superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain
circumstances. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear,
itemised breakdown of what applies to your situation. Before any
commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of
every cost that applies to your situation so there are no
surprises.</span>

### <span class="mark">Conversion CTA headline</span>

<span class="mark">Not sure where to start? We’re here to help.  
Alt  
You don’t need a plan yet. You just need a conversation.</span>

### <span class="mark">FAQs (7 questions)</span>

<span class="mark">The page must answer each of the following:</span>

<span class="mark">**1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight
loss surgery?  **
Not necessarily. A BMI of 30-34 may qualify for a surgical pathway when
weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood
pressure, or sleep apnoea are present. If surgery is not the right
starting point, our medical team can offer meaningful support through
medication and dietitian-led care. The only way to know where you stand
is a proper assessment.</span>

<span class="mark">**2. I have tried every diet and they all failed.
Does that count against me?  **
Not at all. On the contrary, a history of attempts at non-surgical
weight loss is part of what helps confirm that a more intensive pathway
is likely appropriate. It demonstrates that you have tried, not that you
have failed. Obesity is a medical condition and difficulty losing weight
through diet alone is a clinical reality, not a personal
shortcoming.</span>

<span class="mark">**3. Will a mental health history stop me from being
eligible?  **
No. Psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment process, but a
mental health history is not a reason to be turned away. A psychological
review is there to set you up for the best possible outcome and make
sure the timing and support are right for you.</span>

<span class="mark">**4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer
myself?  **
You can contact us directly; a GP referral is not required to get
started. That said, a referral is worth having as it allows Medicare
rebates to apply to your specialist consultation fee, reducing your
out-of-pocket cost for the appointment. If you don't have one yet, our
team can advise you on next steps when you get in touch.</span>

<span class="mark">**5. What actually happens at the first
appointment?  **
Your initial consultation runs for 30-60 minutes and is a relaxed,
honest conversation about your weight history, health, and goals. Your
surgeon explains which options are likely to suit you, walks through the
relevant procedures, and discusses costs openly. You are welcome to ask
as many questions as you like; there is zero obligation to proceed, and
no pressure to decide anything on the day.</span>

<span class="mark">**6. How long does it take to go from first
consultation to surgery?  **
It varies depending on your individual circumstances, the procedure
involved, and how quickly the assessment and preparation stage
progresses. For some patients it is a matter of weeks; for others it
takes longer, particularly if additional tests or team reviews are
needed. Your surgeon will give you a realistic timeframe at your
consultation based on your specific situation.</span>

<span class="mark">**7. What if I find out surgery is not right for
me?  **
You will not leave without options. If surgery is not the right fit —
due to timing, preference, or for clinical reasons — we can discuss
medical weight management pathways including GLP-1 medications and
dietitian-led programs. Some patients start there and revisit surgery
later; others find it is sufficient on its own. The goal is the right
outcome for you, whatever form that takes.</span>

