    60	But eligibility for weight loss surgery is not decided by a single
    61	number. When you meet your Obesity Surgery WA surgeon, they will review
    62	your weight, your whole health history, what you have already tried, and
    63	what you want your life to look like in five or ten years.</span>
    64	
    65	<span class="mark">Technically, body mass index (BMI) is your weight in
    66	kilograms divided by your height in metres squared. As a guide for
    67	Australians: a BMI of ~27 to 29 sits in the overweight range, ~30 to 34
    68	is class 1 obesity, ~35 to 39 is class 2 obesity, and ~40 and above is
    69	class 3, sometimes called severe obesity. Your BMI influences which
    70	options are likely to suit you, but it does not tell the whole story on
    71	its own.</span>
