content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:37:> Draft L228-L246: **How much does a gastric sleeve cost in Perth?**
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:47:> Draft L57-L61: Gastric sleeve surgery is highly effective for weight loss for two key reasons: first, because your stomach is smaller, you feel fuller, faster. Secondly, because a reduced stomach size minimises your body’s production of ghrelin (the ‘hunger hormone’), after gastric sleeve surgery, your appetite will also be reduced.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:63:> Draft L307-L315: **7. Does Medicare cover gastric sleeve surgery?** Medicare item number 31575 attracts a rebate when eligibility criteria are met, offsetting part of the surgical fee. However, Medicare alone doesn't cover the full cost and hospital, theatre, and anaesthetist fees still apply. Private health insurance with a gold-tier (or equivalent) policy will cover the hospital component. A full fee breakdown is available to help you understand your out-of-pocket costs.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:73:> Draft L54-L55: It is typically performed ‘laparoscopically’ via keyhole surgery, which tends to mean an easier recovery than some more invasive procedures.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:77:> Draft L173-L175: Significant, sustained weight loss; an estimated clinical average of 60–70% of excess weight over 12–18 months, though individual results vary.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:81:> Draft L177-L179: Reduced appetite; the procedure reduces stomach size and lowers ghrelin (‘hunger hormone’) production, so smaller portions feel more satisfying.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:85:> Draft L189-L191: Keyhole approach; means less scarring, generally less post-operative pain, and a shorter hospital stay (and recovery time) than open surgery.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:89:> Draft L199-L199: Gastric sleeve surgery is well-established and generally safe, but it is still a major surgery which carries with it some real risks, all of which your surgeon will discuss with you in detail.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:93:> Draft L201-L203: Staple line leak; though it is a serious potential complication and may require further treatment, it only occurs in ~1–3% of cases.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:97:> Draft L205-L207: Reflux or heartburn can occur or (if pre-existing) worsen after a sleeve. In most people it settles, though for others it can be persistent.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:101:> Draft L214-L217: Hair thinning in the months after surgery is typically linked to rapid weight loss and reduced nutritional intake; it’s usually temporary and improves as your intake stabilises.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:105:> Draft L283-L289: **4. How is a gastric sleeve different from a gastric bypass?** A gastric sleeve reduces stomach size but leaves the digestive pathway unchanged. A gastric bypass both reduces the stomach *and* reroutes the small intestine, which can cause some nutrient malabsorption. The bypass may produce slightly greater weight loss and tends to suit patients with significant reflux better.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:109:> Draft L300-L305: **6. I get heartburn already. Is a sleeve still right for me?** Pre-existing reflux is an important factor to raise with your surgeon, as a sleeve can actually worsen symptoms in some patients. For those with significant GORD, a gastric bypass is often the more suitable option as it tends to improve reflux rather than aggravate it.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:131:Why it matters: The draft is 1,844 words including FAQs. Use this cut-list: (1) delete the duplicate gastric-band paragraph, quoted at draft L80-L86, because the sleeve-vs-bypass comparison is already present; (2) delete the standalone "Single permanent procedure; no implanted device to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band" benefit at draft L193-L195 because permanence is covered in the definition and FAQ; (3) after the cost rewrite above, remove the detailed superannuation examples and named-finance sentence at draft L240-L244 and retain one consultation-based funding sentence; and (4) keep each FAQ answer to 3–5 sentences while preserving the new cost, Medicare, 30/30 and long-term-outcomes answers below.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:135:> Draft L80-L86: Another key difference between the gastric sleeve and gastric band is that the sleeve restricts only the amount of food you can consume, not the types of foods (as is the case with the gastric band procedure).
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:139:> Draft L193-L195: Single permanent procedure; no implanted device to adjust or maintain, unlike a gastric band.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:143:> Draft L240-L244: For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, superannuation may be accessible as a funding option in certain circumstances, including: life-threatening conditions, certain psychiatric conditions, and the management of chronic pain. Financing options such as Zip and TLC are also available for eligible patients.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:163:5. **Current Google People-Also-Ask questions checked 10 Jul 2026 ask about the 30/30 eating rule**
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:167:> **5. What does recovery actually look like week to week?** Your first 1-2 days post op are spent in hospital on clear fluids. Week one is rest at home, sipping carefully and managing fatigue. By weeks two to three, your diet usually progresses from fluids to pureed and soft foods. Your dietitian may advise a 30/30 approach—separating food and fluids by about 30 minutes—if this is part of your post-operative plan; follow the instructions tailored to your recovery.
content-reviews/gastric-sleeve-perth.md:169:Why it matters: This writes the missing 30/30 answer into the recovery FAQ without presenting it as a universal rule; the dietitian’s plan remains authoritative.
