docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:227:> You will not leave without options. If surgery is not the right fit — due to timing, preference, or for clinical reasons — we can discuss medical weight management pathways including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs. Some patients start there and revisit surgery later; others find it is sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, whatever form that takes.</span>
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:231:Why it matters: This is the second explicit GLP-1 occurrence after the first-pass fix. The replacement answers who may not be suitable without implying that every reader will find a satisfactory pathway.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:237:> Draft L84: As a general guide, surgical assessment is typically available for those with a BMI of ~35, and almost always from 40 and higher. Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify where weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present and there have been previous attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise, or non-surgical means without lasting success. Please note that these are general clinical indicators only; your surgeon will make a clinical assessment and discuss your options with you when you meet.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:241:Why it matters: “May qualify” is still self-qualification language, and the MBS rule does not create eligibility below BMI 35.
