content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:233:### G2 — MBS-inaccurate BMI wording
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:235:1. **The summary incorrectly places BMI 30-34 on a qualification pathway**
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:239:> As a general guide, MBS eligibility is assessed individually. The relevant thresholds are BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35 or above with a major obesity-related comorbidity, alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; your surgeon will assess your wider health picture and discuss any other clinical pathways.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:241:Why it matters: “May qualify” is still self-qualification language, and the MBS rule does not create eligibility below BMI 35.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:243:2. **The BMI 30-34 bullet implies surgery may be appropriate under MBS**
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:245:> Draft L92-L95: BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: surgery may be appropriate at this range when conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, which can strengthen the case for a surgical pathway.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:247:> BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: this range does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your broader clinical picture and discuss non-MBS options where appropriate; a condition below BMI 35 does not by itself establish eligibility for MBS-funded surgery.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:249:Why it matters: A major comorbidity is relevant to the MBS pathway at BMI 35+, not as a below-35 qualification route.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:251:3. **The BMI 35-39 bullet says other conditions are unnecessary**
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:253:> Draft L97-L100: BMI 35-39: most people in this range are eligible for a surgical assessment, with or without other health conditions. A range of procedures may be suitable, and your surgeon will discuss the options most appropriate for your situation.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:255:> BMI 35-39: MBS eligibility is assessed individually. At BMI 35 or above, the MBS pathway requires a major obesity-related comorbidity alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. Your surgeon will assess your medical history, risks, goals and aftercare readiness before discussing options.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:257:Why it matters: This is the known G2 error. BMI 35+ does not remove the major-comorbidity requirement for MBS eligibility.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:263:> BMI 40+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility or a particular procedure; your surgeon will discuss whether surgery or another pathway is appropriate for you.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:265:Why it matters: BMI 40+ is an MBS indicator, not a promise of eligibility or comparative effectiveness.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:271:> Weight-related health conditions: a major obesity-related comorbidity is relevant to MBS assessment at BMI 35 or above, but a condition does not create MBS eligibility regardless of BMI. Examples may include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH) and severe acid reflux; your surgeon will assess the full clinical picture.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:273:Why it matters: “Regardless of BMI” conflicts with the MBS threshold and should not appear on an eligibility page.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:275:6. **The BMI 30 FAQ repeats the qualification error**
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:280:> **1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight loss surgery?** A BMI of 30-34 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your wider clinical picture and discuss non-surgical medical weight management or dietitian-led care where appropriate; an individual assessment is still required.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:282:Why it matters: The FAQ must answer the reader directly without implying that a comorbidity creates an MBS pathway below BMI 35.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:291:- **Replace the full cost block:** cut Draft L208-L221, including the named finance options and repeated itemised-breakdown sentence, and use the G1 replacement plus links to `/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth` and `/medicare-private-health-cover`.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:292:- **Replace the CTA scaffold:** cut Draft L223-L227 and use one patient-facing heading and one direct link: “### Find out where you stand” followed by “Book a [consultation](/book-consultation) to discuss your eligibility and options.”
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:293:- **Tighten the FAQ block while adding the missing search answers:** remove the editorial instruction at Draft L231, keep existing high-value questions to 2-3 sentences, and replace the current final FAQ block from Draft L284-L291 with the paste-ready replacement below. This adds cost in Perth, Medicare coverage, the 30/30 rule, who may not be suitable and 10-year outcomes without expanding the finance section.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:303:> **9. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery?** Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but Medicare does not cover every cost and no rebate should be assumed. The applicable item depends on the procedure—31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch—so your surgeon and insurer should confirm what applies.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:305:> **10. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?** Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:307:> **11. What can happen over 10 years after weight loss surgery?** Long-term experiences vary. Some people maintain changes, while others regain weight, develop nutritional issues or need further treatment; ongoing medical, dietary and supplement follow-up may remain important. Your surgeon can discuss the evidence and what your health and follow-up plan may mean for you, but no single 10-year outcome can be promised.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:309:The verified destination slugs available in `content-briefs/` are: `/gastric-sleeve-perth`, `/gastric-bypass-perth`, `/non-surgical-weight-loss`, `/medicare-private-health-cover`, `/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth`, `/book-consultation`, `/gp-referral`, `/faqs`, `/hospitals-locations` and `/surgeons`.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:315:> You can [contact the practice](/book-consultation) directly or ask your GP for a [referral](/gp-referral). A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to current requirements; ask Medicare and the practice what applies.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:319:> Your bariatric surgeon will explain which options may suit you, including [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [non-surgical weight loss support](/non-surgical-weight-loss) or another pathway. Costs and next steps are discussed at consultation without pressure to proceed.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:321:> Draft L208-L221: The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain circumstances. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of what applies to your situation. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of every cost that applies to your situation so there are no surprises.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:323:> The [cost of weight loss surgery in Perth](/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth) varies by procedure and individual circumstances. Read our [Medicare and private health cover guide](/medicare-private-health-cover), then ask the team for an itemised estimate at consultation; coverage and out-of-pocket costs must be confirmed with the relevant providers.
content-reviews/am-i-eligible.md:349:> The hardest part is often making the first call. [Book a consultation](/book-consultation) to discuss your eligibility and options.
