docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:255:> BMI 35-39: MBS eligibility is assessed individually. At BMI 35 or above, the MBS pathway requires a major obesity-related comorbidity alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. Your surgeon will assess your medical history, risks, goals and aftercare readiness before discussing options.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:257:Why it matters: This is the known G2 error. BMI 35+ does not remove the major-comorbidity requirement for MBS eligibility.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:263:> BMI 40+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility or a particular procedure; your surgeon will discuss whether surgery or another pathway is appropriate for you.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:265:Why it matters: BMI 40+ is an MBS indicator, not a promise of eligibility or comparative effectiveness.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:271:> Weight-related health conditions: a major obesity-related comorbidity is relevant to MBS assessment at BMI 35 or above, but a condition does not create MBS eligibility regardless of BMI. Examples may include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH) and severe acid reflux; your surgeon will assess the full clinical picture.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:273:Why it matters: “Regardless of BMI” conflicts with the MBS threshold and should not appear on an eligibility page.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:275:6. **The BMI 30 FAQ repeats the qualification error**
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:280:> **1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight loss surgery?** A BMI of 30-34 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your wider clinical picture and discuss non-surgical medical weight management or dietitian-led care where appropriate; an individual assessment is still required.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:282:Why it matters: The FAQ must answer the reader directly without implying that a comorbidity creates an MBS pathway below BMI 35.
