docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:293:- **Tighten the FAQ block while adding the missing search answers:** remove the editorial instruction at Draft L231, keep existing high-value questions to 2-3 sentences, and replace the current final FAQ block from Draft L284-L291 with the paste-ready replacement below. This adds cost in Perth, Medicare coverage, the 30/30 rule, who may not be suitable and 10-year outcomes without expanding the finance section.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:303:> **9. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery?** Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but Medicare does not cover every cost and no rebate should be assumed. The applicable item depends on the procedure—31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch—so your surgeon and insurer should confirm what applies.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:305:> **10. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?** Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:307:> **11. What can happen over 10 years after weight loss surgery?** Long-term experiences vary. Some people maintain changes, while others regain weight, develop nutritional issues or need further treatment; ongoing medical, dietary and supplement follow-up may remain important. Your surgeon can discuss the evidence and what your health and follow-up plan may mean for you, but no single 10-year outcome can be promised.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:309:The verified destination slugs available in `content-briefs/` are: `/gastric-sleeve-perth`, `/gastric-bypass-perth`, `/non-surgical-weight-loss`, `/medicare-private-health-cover`, `/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth`, `/book-consultation`, `/gp-referral`, `/faqs`, `/hospitals-locations` and `/surgeons`.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:315:> You can [contact the practice](/book-consultation) directly or ask your GP for a [referral](/gp-referral). A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to current requirements; ask Medicare and the practice what applies.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:319:> Your bariatric surgeon will explain which options may suit you, including [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [non-surgical weight loss support](/non-surgical-weight-loss) or another pathway. Costs and next steps are discussed at consultation without pressure to proceed.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:321:> Draft L208-L221: The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain circumstances. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of what applies to your situation. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of every cost that applies to your situation so there are no surprises.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:323:> The [cost of weight loss surgery in Perth](/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth) varies by procedure and individual circumstances. Read our [Medicare and private health cover guide](/medicare-private-health-cover), then ask the team for an itemised estimate at consultation; coverage and out-of-pocket costs must be confirmed with the relevant providers.
docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/review/am-i-eligible.md:349:> The hardest part is often making the first call. [Book a consultation](/book-consultation) to discuss your eligibility and options.
