 Summary: Verdict is REWORK because the draft has compliance-sensitive eligibility language, visible editorial scaffolding, finance drift, prescription-class wording and a major word-count overage.
 Summary: The core medical structure is strong, but revise BMI/mental-health/safety claims into assessment-criteria language and add a direct "who is not eligible" answer.
 Summary: Cut the cost/finance section, duplicate FAQ material, alternates and padding to bring the page back toward 1,000-1,300 words including FAQs.
+
+## Batch-1 gap-closing additions (G1-G5)
+
+Source line references below refer to `docs/oswa/content-review-2026-07/extracted/am-i-eligible.md`.
+
+### G1 — residual compliance claims
+
+1. **The FAQ repeats an unqualified mental-health assurance and an outcome promise**
+
+> Draft L251-L256: **3. Will a mental health history stop me from being eligible?  **
+> No. Psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment process, but a mental health history is not a reason to be turned away. A psychological review is there to set you up for the best possible outcome and make sure the timing and support are right for you.
+
+> **3. Will a mental health history stop me from being eligible?** A mental health history does not automatically rule out surgery. Psychological wellbeing is part of the assessment, and active or unstable concerns, an untreated eating disorder, substance misuse or difficulty following aftercare may affect timing or suitability. Your surgeon will discuss what support or further review may be needed.
+
+Why it matters: The FAQ is a second occurrence after the eligibility bullet. It must not promise that a mental-health history will never affect suitability or that review will produce a particular outcome.
+
+2. **The hospital bullet uses a comparative marketing claim**
+
+> Draft L172-L174: Care from MBBS FRACS-certified bariatric surgeons, with procedures performed at three leading private hospital locations in Perth.
+
+> Care from bariatric surgeons with relevant specialist credentials, with procedures performed at Perth hospital locations confirmed with your surgeon. Verify the credentials and hospital affiliations before publication.
+
+Why it matters: “Leading” is a comparative claim, and the credential wording should not imply a certification that has not been verified.
+
+3. **The risks section calls surgery a “powerful tool”**
+
+> Draft L202-L204: Surgery is a powerful tool, but it works best when supported by the habits and care built around it — it is not a standalone cure.
+
+> Surgery is one treatment option with ongoing eating, nutrition and follow-up requirements. Your surgeon and care team will explain the likely benefits, limits and risks for your circumstances; outcomes vary between individuals.
+
+Why it matters: Keep the “not a cure” clarification without making a broad effectiveness claim.
+
+4. **The cost paragraph promises a significant level of private-health cover**
+
+> Draft L208-L216: The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain circumstances.
+
+> The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies by procedure, hospital, clinician fees, private-health policy, waiting periods, excess and individual circumstances. Medicare or private-health benefits may apply only when the relevant requirements are met; ask Medicare and your insurer to confirm what applies. Any applicable range or estimate is discussed at consultation, and the team should provide an itemised estimate without promising coverage or naming finance providers.
+
+Why it matters: “A significant portion ... is covered” and named funding options can read as a coverage or access promise. Keep the funding explanation measured and consultation-based.
+
+5. **The GP-referral FAQ makes a second rebate and out-of-pocket claim**
+
+> Draft L258-L264: **4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer myself?  **
+> You can contact us directly; a GP referral is not required to get started. That said, a referral is worth having as it allows Medicare rebates to apply to your specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the appointment. If you don't have one yet, our team can advise you on next steps when you get in touch.
+
+> **4. Do I need a GP referral, or can I refer myself?** You can contact the practice directly; a GP referral is not required to get started. A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to current requirements, but the practice and Medicare should confirm what applies to your appointment and fees.
+
+Why it matters: A referral should not be presented as guaranteed rebate access or a guaranteed reduction in the patient's cost.
+
+6. **The FAQ contains a secondary GLP-1 reference**
+
+> Draft L284-L291: **7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?  **
+> You will not leave without options. If surgery is not the right fit — due to timing, preference, or for clinical reasons — we can discuss medical weight management pathways including GLP-1 medications and dietitian-led programs. Some patients start there and revisit surgery later; others find it is sufficient on its own. The goal is the right outcome for you, whatever form that takes.</span>
+
+> **7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?** Some people are not suitable for surgery straight away, or may not be suitable at all. Reasons can include a medical condition that makes anaesthetic or surgery too risky, pregnancy, active substance misuse, an untreated eating disorder or difficulty safely following the required aftercare. Your surgeon will explain the assessment and discuss non-surgical medical weight management or dietitian-led support where appropriate. Further medication wording remains subject to the pending TGA/legal review; do not add brand names.
+
+Why it matters: This is the second explicit GLP-1 occurrence after the first-pass fix. The replacement answers who may not be suitable without implying that every reader will find a satisfactory pathway.
+
+### G2 — MBS-inaccurate BMI wording
+
+1. **The summary incorrectly places BMI 30-34 on a qualification pathway**
+
+> Draft L84: As a general guide, surgical assessment is typically available for those with a BMI of ~35, and almost always from 40 and higher. Someone with a BMI of 30 to 34 may qualify where weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present and there have been previous attempts at weight loss through diet, exercise, or non-surgical means without lasting success. Please note that these are general clinical indicators only; your surgeon will make a clinical assessment and discuss your options with you when you meet.
+
+> As a general guide, MBS eligibility is assessed individually. The relevant thresholds are BMI 40 or above, or BMI 35 or above with a major obesity-related comorbidity, alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. A BMI below 35 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items; your surgeon will assess your wider health picture and discuss any other clinical pathways.
+
+Why it matters: “May qualify” is still self-qualification language, and the MBS rule does not create eligibility below BMI 35.
+
+2. **The BMI 30-34 bullet implies surgery may be appropriate under MBS**
+
+> Draft L92-L95: BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: surgery may be appropriate at this range when conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present, which can strengthen the case for a surgical pathway.
+
+> BMI 30-34 with a weight-related health condition: this range does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your broader clinical picture and discuss non-MBS options where appropriate; a condition below BMI 35 does not by itself establish eligibility for MBS-funded surgery.
+
+Why it matters: A major comorbidity is relevant to the MBS pathway at BMI 35+, not as a below-35 qualification route.
+
+3. **The BMI 35-39 bullet says other conditions are unnecessary**
+
+> Draft L97-L100: BMI 35-39: most people in this range are eligible for a surgical assessment, with or without other health conditions. A range of procedures may be suitable, and your surgeon will discuss the options most appropriate for your situation.
+
+> BMI 35-39: MBS eligibility is assessed individually. At BMI 35 or above, the MBS pathway requires a major obesity-related comorbidity alongside the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. Your surgeon will assess your medical history, risks, goals and aftercare readiness before discussing options.
+
+Why it matters: This is the known G2 error. BMI 35+ does not remove the major-comorbidity requirement for MBS eligibility.
+
+4. **The BMI 40+ bullet turns an assessment indicator into an eligibility and effectiveness claim**
+
+> Draft L102-L105: BMI 40+: almost everyone in this range is eligible for surgery and for many patients it represents the most effective long-term pathway available. Your surgeon can confirm which procedure is the right fit.
+
+> BMI 40+: MBS eligibility is assessed individually against the applicable item, clinical and referral requirements. BMI alone does not guarantee eligibility or a particular procedure; your surgeon will discuss whether surgery or another pathway is appropriate for you.
+
+Why it matters: BMI 40+ is an MBS indicator, not a promise of eligibility or comparative effectiveness.
+
+5. **The comorbidity bullet says a condition can strengthen a case regardless of BMI**
+
+> Draft L107-L111: Weight-related health conditions or comorbidities: certain conditions strengthen a surgical case regardless of BMI. These include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH), and severe acid reflux.
+
+> Weight-related health conditions: a major obesity-related comorbidity is relevant to MBS assessment at BMI 35 or above, but a condition does not create MBS eligibility regardless of BMI. Examples may include type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, obstructive sleep apnoea, severe osteoarthritis, PCOS, fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH) and severe acid reflux; your surgeon will assess the full clinical picture.
+
+Why it matters: “Regardless of BMI” conflicts with the MBS threshold and should not appear on an eligibility page.
+
+6. **The BMI 30 FAQ repeats the qualification error**
+
+> Draft L233-L240: **1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight loss surgery?  **
+> Not necessarily. A BMI of 30-34 may qualify for a surgical pathway when weight-related health conditions such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea are present. If surgery is not the right starting point, our medical team can offer meaningful support through medication and dietitian-led care. The only way to know where you stand is a proper assessment.
+
+> **1. My BMI is around 30; am I too low for weight loss surgery?** A BMI of 30-34 does not meet the MBS threshold for these items. Your surgeon can assess your wider clinical picture and discuss non-surgical medical weight management or dietitian-led care where appropriate; an individual assessment is still required.
+
+Why it matters: The FAQ must answer the reader directly without implying that a comorbidity creates an MBS pathway below BMI 35.
