+- **Keep the process explanation and shorten the duplicate FAQ:** retain Draft L133-L139 as the process copy, but replace Draft L266-L273, beginning “Your initial consultation runs for 30-60 minutes...” and ending “no pressure to decide anything on the day.”, with: “At your first appointment, your surgeon reviews your weight history, health conditions, goals and questions, then explains which pathways may be suitable. Costs and next steps are discussed without pressure to proceed.”
+- **Replace the full cost block:** cut Draft L208-L221, including the named finance options and repeated itemised-breakdown sentence, and use the G1 replacement plus links to `/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth` and `/medicare-private-health-cover`.
+- **Replace the CTA scaffold:** cut Draft L223-L227 and use one patient-facing heading and one direct link: “### Find out where you stand” followed by “Book a [consultation](/book-consultation) to discuss your eligibility and options.”
+- **Tighten the FAQ block while adding the missing search answers:** remove the editorial instruction at Draft L231, keep existing high-value questions to 2-3 sentences, and replace the current final FAQ block from Draft L284-L291 with the paste-ready replacement below. This adds cost in Perth, Medicare coverage, the 30/30 rule, who may not be suitable and 10-year outcomes without expanding the finance section.
+
+#### Paste-ready FAQ replacement
+
+Replace Draft L284-L291 with:
+
+> **7. What if I find out surgery is not right for me?** Some people are not suitable for surgery straight away, or may not be suitable at all. Reasons can include a medical condition that makes anaesthetic or surgery too risky, pregnancy, active substance misuse, an untreated eating disorder or difficulty safely following the required aftercare. Your surgeon will explain the assessment and discuss non-surgical options where appropriate.
+>
+> **8. How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?** There is no single Perth price. Costs vary by procedure, hospital, surgeon, anaesthetist, tests, private-health cover, excess, waiting periods and individual circumstances. Any applicable range or estimate is discussed at consultation, with an itemised breakdown before you decide.
+>
+> **9. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery?** Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but Medicare does not cover every cost and no rebate should be assumed. The applicable item depends on the procedure—31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch—so your surgeon and insurer should confirm what applies.
+>
+> **10. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?** Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
+>
+> **11. What can happen over 10 years after weight loss surgery?** Long-term experiences vary. Some people maintain changes, while others regain weight, develop nutritional issues or need further treatment; ongoing medical, dietary and supplement follow-up may remain important. Your surgeon can discuss the evidence and what your health and follow-up plan may mean for you, but no single 10-year outcome can be promised.
+
+The verified destination slugs available in `content-briefs/` are: `/gastric-sleeve-perth`, `/gastric-bypass-perth`, `/non-surgical-weight-loss`, `/medicare-private-health-cover`, `/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth`, `/book-consultation`, `/gp-referral`, `/faqs`, `/hospitals-locations` and `/surgeons`.
+
+Use these paste-ready link placements:
+
+> Draft L129-L131: You can contact us directly or ask your GP to send a referral. A GP referral can help with Medicare rebates on consultation fees so it is worth asking for one, but it is not required to get started.
+
+> You can [contact the practice](/book-consultation) directly or ask your GP for a [referral](/gp-referral). A referral may allow a Medicare rebate for an eligible specialist consultation, subject to current requirements; ask Medicare and the practice what applies.
+
+> Draft L135-L139: Your MBBS FRACS surgeon will explain which options are likely to suit you, walk through relevant procedures, and answer your questions. Costs are discussed openly so you leave knowing exactly where you stand, with no obligation to proceed.
+
+> Your bariatric surgeon will explain which options may suit you, including [gastric sleeve surgery](/gastric-sleeve-perth), [gastric bypass surgery](/gastric-bypass-perth), [non-surgical weight loss support](/non-surgical-weight-loss) or another pathway. Costs and next steps are discussed at consultation without pressure to proceed.
+
+> Draft L208-L221: The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain circumstances. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of what applies to your situation. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear, itemised breakdown of every cost that applies to your situation so there are no surprises.
+
+> The [cost of weight loss surgery in Perth](/weight-loss-surgery-cost-perth) varies by procedure and individual circumstances. Read our [Medicare and private health cover guide](/medicare-private-health-cover), then ask the team for an itemised estimate at consultation; coverage and out-of-pocket costs must be confirmed with the relevant providers.
+
+Why it matters: The cut-list names the exact blocks to remove or compress, and each internal link points to a real destination slug rather than a bare “here” anchor.
